        "answer": "# Generic GPU Evidence Procedure — MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF

Source: MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (mosaic-gpu-validation-20261031 collection), authored by Jeff Peng, reference MOSAIC-GPU-KB-20261031.

---

## 1. Overview

This document supplies a general diagnostic procedure for assessing GPU error evidence without performing repairs [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)]. It establishes a structured evidence-collection order and a set of decision rules for distinguishing simulated from live hardware telemetry.

## 2. Evidence Collection Order

The prescribed initial evidence order is:

1. Map GPU index to UUID and PCI bus ID.
2. Retain driver and DCGM versions and timestamps.
3. Collect `nvidia-smi -q -x` (XML dump).
4. Collect compute-process inventory.
5. Collect relevant kernel NVRM/Xid/PCIe AER records.
6. Collect existing DCGM/Prometheus observations.
7. State the observed time window and any inaccessible or rotated logs [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)].

This ordered procedure ensures that the foundational identity and version context is recorded before interpreting any error indicators.

## 3. Xid 95

Xid 95 denotes an uncontained memory error [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)]. The document classifies it as a serious error classification. However, an Xid 95 by itself is not proof of a particular failed memory chip, bank, or replaceable card [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)]. For a genuine incident, the applicable NVIDIA recovery guidance and the site maintenance procedure should be followed [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)]. Additionally, the GPU must not be reset during a read-only investigation [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)].

## 4. Volatile Versus Aggregate ECC

The document prescribes reading volatile and aggregate ECC counts separately [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)]. Key points:

- A nonzero lifetime aggregate count alone does not prove errors are growing currently [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)].
- Compare timestamped samples to determine whether errors are active or historical [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)].
- Preserve the SRAM versus DRAM distinction and exact counter labels [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)].
- Check remapped-row pending/failure and spare-bank information [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)].
- Keep N/A separate from numeric zero — they represent different states [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)].

This distinction prevents the common mistake of treating a cumulative lifetime aggregate as evidence of an ongoing fault.

## 5. Isolated DCGM Cache Versus Live Hardware Evidence

The document establishes a critical separation between DCGM test-injection cache snapshots and independent hardware evidence [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)]:

- DCGM test injection changes samples in a host-engine cache [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)].
- Synthetic Xid or ECC samples from one isolated engine are not independent NVML or kernel evidence [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)].
- The source must be labeled `isolated_dcgm_cache_snapshot` [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)].
- A Kubernetes pod serving that snapshot is a telemetry replay fixture; its CPU resources do not make the observed signal a CPU application fault [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)].

When injected DCGM samples disagree with unchanged live NVML ECC/remap counters and no matching kernel Xid is found in the available log window, the report should state that this is simulated GPU telemetry with insufficient evidence of a live hardware defect [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)]. The injection or fixture must be identified as the cause of the test alert [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)]. Injected counters must not be called corroboration of a physical failure [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)].

Additional procedural constraints for test engines:
- Keep test engines on private sockets [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)].
- Never inject into a production engine shared with health monitoring or remediation [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)].
- Never globally pause monitoring [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)].
- Preserve exact command, GPU identity, engine endpoint, field ID, value, timestamp, tool output and cleanup evidence [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)].
- Terminate only the test engine that the run created [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)].

## 6. Kernel-Log Limitations

The procedure requires collecting "relevant kernel NVRM/Xid/PCIe AER records" and explicitly instructs the investigator to "state the observed time window and inaccessible or rotated logs" [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)]. This establishes that kernel logs may be incomplete due to rotation, and that the time window covered by available logs must be declared alongside the evidence [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)]. Furthermore, if no matching kernel Xid is found within the available log window, that is reported as a gap rather than proof of absence [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)].

Additionally, the document notes that a missing Prometheus Xid/ECC series is an instrumentation gap, not a successful zero-error measurement [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)]. Before claiming absence of a fault, one must verify the exporter collector list, field support, versions, scrape targets and exact metric names [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)].

## 7. Public Retrieval Verification Code (Page 2)

The public retrieval verification code on page 2 is:

**GPU-CEDAR-7304**

The document states: "This marker grants no access. Retrieve it only from this file and cite page 2" [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)].

## 8. Primary Reference Sources

The document cites three primary NVIDIA source URLs (checked 2026-10-31) [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)]:

- https://docs.nvidia.com/datacenter/dcgm/latest/learn/dcgm-error-injection.html
- https://docs.nvidia.com/datacenter/dcgm/latest/reference/command-line-reference/dcgmi/dcgmi-test.html
- https://docs.nvidia.com/deploy/xid-errors/analyzing-xid-catalog.html

These reference URLs support error semantics and cache-injection boundaries but the document itself does not reproduce the manuals [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)].

## 9. Scope and Limitations

The document is authored as a test runbook and procedure, not as incident evidence [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)]. It contains no observations about any current GPU, host, alert, or experiment [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 1)]. View mode permits evidence collection and recommendations only; a completed analysis does not establish repair, RMA eligibility, or Feishu receipt [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)].

Research sources provide generic procedure only; the operator or agent must obtain incident facts from actual tools and logs, cite those separately, and state uncertainty [MOSAIC-GPU-EVIDENCE-RUNBOOK.PDF (Page 2)].

